WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.660
This is a deep-sea anglerfish mating pair.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.860
These animals have rarely been observed

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.180
in their natural habitat

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.360
-- hundreds to thousands of meters

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.470
below the ocean’s surface.

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:15.470
You’re watching one of the few known videos.

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:18.620
And this one shows a sexually parasitic pair

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.780
for the first time.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:22.140
That means during mating,

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.580
the tiny male is attached to the female.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:28.960
He gets nutrients and she gets sperm.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.360
These specialized fish rarely survive

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.260
the journey to the surface.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.080
Before now, most deep sea anglerfish studies

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.240
used dead animals pulled up in nets

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.780
and sometimes preserved in museums.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.580
In this case, the research team used

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:50.920
a special submersible

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.829
designed to capture images of creatures

00:00:52.829 --> 00:00:54.860
that live at these great depths.

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:58.560
Like most other members of its order,

00:00:58.579 --> 00:01:01.670
this anglerfish is known for the bioluminescent, lure-like

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:04.780
appendage that drifts in front of its mouth

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.200
to attract prey.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.020
But this is the first time

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:10.480
that what may be bioluminescence

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.990
on the fish’s filaments has been documented.

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:16.360
The swaying swarm of thin projections

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:17.420
 

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.000
may act like a 3-d array of cat whiskers

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.560
- sensing dangers and prey in the environment.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:27.180
There’s little to eat at this depth

00:01:27.189 --> 00:01:28.960
and it is very cold -

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.479
leaving anglerfish with few calories to spare

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:35.240
so it makes sense that as this video shows,

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:38.710
the anglerfish female seems to use very little energy--

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:42.060
slowly drifting and rolling through the water.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.890
But we don’t know much about their metabolism,

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:47.290
the bacteria that supply their glow,

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:51.220
or how the female’s body doesn’t reject the male’s.

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:54.540
The special ship that took this video has

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.260
been in operation since 2013

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.560
and there aren’t many like it in the world.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.159
So chances are,

00:02:01.159 --> 00:02:03.640
many anglerfish mysteries will stay that way

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:04.880
for some time to come.

